Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `event_date`: filter to 20201101‑20201130 and group by day.  
• `event_name`: pick out ‘page_view’ events and ‘purchase’ events.  
• `user_pseudo_id`: identify / de‑duplicate users, join the two sets above.  
Extra conditions we will use  
• `_TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20201101' AND '20201130'` – limits the wildcard read to November 2020.  
• `event_name = 'purchase'` – users that bought.  
• `event_name = 'page_view'` – page‑view events.  

Below are 10 exploratory → final SQL statements (all capped with `LIMIT 20`).  
Replace nothing – the project, dataset and table names are already correct.

```sql
-- 1. Distinct event names that occur on 5‑Nov‑2020
SELECT DISTINCT event_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201105`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. First 20 page‑view rows on 5‑Nov‑2020
SELECT event_timestamp, user_pseudo_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201105`
WHERE event_name = 'page_view'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. First 20 purchase rows on 5‑Nov‑2020
SELECT event_timestamp, user_pseudo_id, ecommerce.transaction_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201105`
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Purchases per day in November 2020 (first 20 days)
SELECT _TABLE_SUFFIX AS event_date, COUNT(*) AS purchase_events
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202011*`
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
GROUP BY event_date
ORDER BY event_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Sample list of distinct users who bought something in Nov‑2020
SELECT DISTINCT user_pseudo_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202011*`
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Inspect event_params of one purchase event
SELECT ep.key, ep.value.string_value, ep.value.int_value
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201105`,
UNNEST(event_params) AS ep
WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Inspect event_params of one page_view event
SELECT ep.key, ep.value.string_value, ep.value.int_value
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201105`,
UNNEST(event_params) AS ep
WHERE event_name = 'page_view'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Page views per user on 10‑Nov‑2020 (sample 20 users)
SELECT user_pseudo_id,
       COUNT(*) AS page_views
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_20201110`
WHERE event_name = 'page_view'
GROUP BY user_pseudo_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Page views per user across the whole of November 2020 (sample 20)
SELECT user_pseudo_id,
       COUNT(*) AS nov_page_views
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202011*`
WHERE event_name = 'page_view'
GROUP BY user_pseudo_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: total page views per day in Nov‑2020
--       + average page views per user
WITH purchasers AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT user_pseudo_id
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202011*`
  WHERE event_name = 'purchase'
),
daily_views AS (
  SELECT
    event_date,
    user_pseudo_id
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ga4_obfuscated_sample_ecommerce.events_202011*`
  WHERE event_name = 'page_view'
    AND user_pseudo_id IN (SELECT user_pseudo_id FROM purchasers)
)
SELECT
  event_date,
  COUNT(*)                                   AS total_page_views,
  COUNT(DISTINCT user_pseudo_id)             AS users_with_purchase,
  COUNT(*) / COUNT(DISTINCT user_pseudo_id)  AS avg_page_views_per_user
FROM daily_views
GROUP BY event_date
ORDER BY event_date
LIMIT 20;
```
